The recursive call returns the root of the updated subtree. That returned pointer is assigned back to `left` or `right`, so the same code works when the tree is empty and when a new node is added below an existing node.

```javascript
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * class TreeNode {
 *      constructor(val = 0, left = null, right = null){
 *          this.data = val;
 *          this.left = null;
 *          this.right = null;
 *      }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    insertIntoBST(root, val) {
        if (root === null) {
            return new TreeNode(val);
        }

        if (val < root.data) {
            root.left = this.insertIntoBST(root.left, val);
        } else {
            root.right = this.insertIntoBST(root.right, val);
        }

        return root;
    }
}
```

A short dry run for a tree containing `8` and `3`, inserting `5`: `5` is smaller than `8`, so the call goes left. It is larger than `3`, so it goes right and creates the missing child there.

If the value already exists, this version sends it right. The tree nodes are changed in place, and the returned root is still the same unless the input root was null.

Time complexity: `O(h)`, with `h` equal to the height of the tree.
Space complexity: `O(h)` because of the call stack.

An iterative walk is enough here. Keep moving left or right until the matching child pointer is empty, then attach the new node there. The root itself only changes when the original tree is empty.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public TreeNode insertIntoBST(TreeNode root, int val) {
        TreeNode newNode = new TreeNode(val);

        if (root == null) {
            return newNode;
        }

        TreeNode current = root;

        while (true) {
            if (val < current.data) {
                if (current.left == null) {
                    current.left = newNode;
                    break;
                }
                current = current.left;
            } else {
                if (current.right == null) {
                    current.right = newNode;
                    break;
                }
                current = current.right;
            }
        }

        return root;
    }
}
```

A quick dry run with values `5, 3, 7`, followed by `6`: the walk checks `5`, goes right at `5`, then goes left at `7`, and attaches `6` as the left child of `7`.

No input values are changed, but the tree links are updated in place. The loop uses constant extra memory.

Time complexity: `O(h)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The recursive version follows the BST rule directly. Smaller values move left, and larger values move right. When an empty spot is found, a new node is created there.

For example, inserting `6` into a tree rooted at `5` moves right. If the right child is `8`, it moves left from there until the correct empty link is reached.

